Root cause:
The bug occurs when the kernel attempts to execute code related to userfaultfd write protection (uffd-wp) even when the `PTE_MARKER_UFFD_WP` feature is not configured. This leads to warnings being triggered because the code paths that handle pte markers are reached without the necessary support being compiled in.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (unlikely(!pte_present(pte))) {
    ...
    if (pte_swp_uffd_wp_any(pte) &&
        !(zap_flags & ZAP_FLAG_DROP_MARKER))
        set_huge_pte_at(mm, address, ptep,
                         make_pte_marker(PTE_MARKER_UFFD_WP));
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the code attempts to execute the logic for handling userfaultfd write protection (uffd-wp) markers even when the `PTE_MARKER_UFFD_WP` feature is not compiled in. This leads to warnings when the code paths that reference `PTE_MARKER_UFFD_WP` are reached without the necessary support.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
#ifdef CONFIG_PTE_MARKER_UFFD_WP
    ...
#endif
```
Reason:
The patch adds preprocessor conditionals (`#ifdef CONFIG_PTE_MARKER_UFFD_WP`) around the code that handles the uffd-wp markers. This ensures that the related code is only compiled and executed if the `PTE_MARKER_UFFD_WP` feature is enabled, preventing the warnings from being triggered when the feature is not configured.


